From 0fc274e8db62b454ca7d1cd6107ae15d8fb5ef7a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: andrew <andrew@rstltd.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 09:47:45 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] preceph: accept Bias-SINEX records without a std-dev
 column

readbiaf() requires each record to be at least 91 characters, which
demands a trailing _STD_DEV___ field. Records that stop after
__ESTIMATED_VALUE____ are shorter and get skipped, with the same silent
outcome as a mis-parsed column: the file loads "successfully" and every
bias reads back as 0.

Nothing the parser indexes needs the line to be that long. The last
field it touches is the value at column 70, and str2num() already clamps
to the end of the line -- a short or absent value yields 0, which the
existing

    if ((cbias=str2num(buff,70,21))==0.0) continue;

check skips. 71 is enough to guarantee the value field has begun.

This keeps the blank-SVN handling from 5b59da3 intact; it only stops
requiring an optional trailing column. Reproduced with an 88-character
OSB record (C1C = 5 ns), which is skipped before this change and loads
after it.

Separable from the OBS2 column fix in the previous commit -- drop this
one if you consider the std-dev field mandatory for this reader.
---
 src/preceph.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/preceph.c b/src/preceph.c
index 11b262d9..af3dd95c 100644
--- a/src/preceph.c
+++ b/src/preceph.c
@@ -476,7 +476,7 @@ static int readbiaf(const char *file, nav_t *nav)
         return 0;
     }
     while (fgets(buff,sizeof(buff),fp)) {
-        if ((int)strlen(buff)<91) continue;
+        if ((int)strlen(buff)<71) continue;
         strncpy(bias, buff+1,  3); bias[3] ='\0';
         strncpy(prn,  buff+11, 3); prn[3]  ='\0';
         strncpy(obs1, buff+25, 3); obs1[3] ='\0';
